GREEN — all 14 assertions pass, no assertion failures.

## What changed

**`format.js`** — one new guard-clause block, placed after the case transforms and before `prefix`/`suffix`:

```js
if (options.truncate && result.length > options.truncate) {
  const ellipsis = "...";
  const budget = options.truncate - ellipsis.length;

  if (budget <= 0) {
    result = ellipsis.slice(0, options.truncate);
  } else {
    const head = result.slice(0, budget);
    const atWordBoundary = head.replace(/\s+\S*$/, "");
    result = (atWordBoundary || head.trimEnd()) + ellipsis;
  }
}
```

The `||` is the hard-cut fallback: when the budget contains no usable word boundary, `replace` yields an empty string and the raw head is used instead.

**`format.test.js`** — 9 `console.assert` lines in the existing style, covering under-limit, exactly-at-limit, word-boundary cut, no-boundary hard cut, trailing-whitespace trim, sub-ellipsis budget, and ordering against `uppercase`/`prefix`/`suffix`.

## Verification

`node format.test.js` — clean, all assertions pass. I watched the 7 new behavioral assertions fail first for the right reason (option not implemented).

## Two things worth knowing

- **`node format.test.js` prints a `MODULE_TYPELESS_PACKAGE_JSON` warning.** Pre-existing — it appeared in the RED run too. `package.json` has no `"type": "module"` field. One line fixes it, but it's unrelated to this change so I left it alone.
- **`format.test.js` prints "All tests passed" unconditionally.** `console.assert` logs and continues rather than throwing, so the final line printed even during the RED run with 7 failures. It's a pre-existing property of the harness, not something this change introduced, but it means the file can't be trusted as a pass/fail signal in CI — you have to read the output. Say the word if you want that tightened up.
